3c) Method: An iniƟal temperature is assumed for all the nodes, say 50, and is
used to calculate the first iteraƟon of nodal temperatures using the finite
difference equaƟons. The wall is assumed to be insulated on all four sides ,
while a spaƟally varying internal heat generaƟon term is included at every

node. The temperatures of the cooling coil locaƟons are fixed at the user-
specified values (35°C) during every iteraƟon and are not updated. The first

iteraƟon is stored in the list Tn.
A funcƟon step() performs all subsequent iteraƟons. At the beginning of each
iteraƟon, a copy of the current nodal temperatures is stored as Told. Using Told,
all corner, boundary and interior node temperatures are recalculated by
considering conducƟon in both x- and y-direcƟons, internal heat generaƟon
and the cooling coil constraints, and the updated temperatures are stored in Tn.
The maximum absolute difference between the corresponding elements of Tn
and Told is then calculated. If the maximum difference is less than 0.001, the
temperatures are considered to have converged, and the final nodal
temperatures, number of iteraƟons and the temperature distribuƟon along the
mid-horizontal line of the wall are ploƩed. Otherwise, the iteraƟons conƟnue
unƟl convergence or the maximum iteraƟon limit is reached.

